# Rank 1708 / CVE-2026-22043: RustFS's policy engine evaluated
# `deny_only` requests by skipping the Allow pass and returning allowed.
# `Policy::is_allowed` (crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs:66 at 356dc7e^)
# ran the Deny statements and then read
# `if args.deny_only || args.is_owner { return true; }`, so any caller that
# asked for a deny-only evaluation is granted access with no matching Allow
# statement. Three admin handlers reached that path on a self-service
# condition rather than on a permission: AddServiceAccount
# (rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs:115) derived the flag from
# `constant_time_eq(&cred.access_key, &target_user) ||
# constant_time_eq(&cred.parent_user, &target_user)`, and AddUser and
# GetUserInfo (rustfs/src/admin/handlers/user.rs:121, :378) from
# `ak == cred.access_key`. A restricted service account whose parent matches
# the target therefore passed the gate without holding
# CreateServiceAccountAdminAction / CreateUserAdminAction and minted an
# unrestricted credential (CWE-269, reported by Positive Technologies as
# PT-2026-2144).
#
# The row names a commit that a95e549430851f3c34ee4cc8dd7be60aa200e8c4
# is the peeled commit of the 1.0.0-alpha.79 release tag the advisory names,
# and it is not the fixing change -- it is an audit-registry and TCP-keepalive cleanup
# whose parent contains the remediation (356dc7e is an ancestor of
# a95e549, five commits earlier). The fixing change for the deny_only short-circuit is
# 356dc7e0c2b4438ef3b447160003ea4571fb99ad ("feat: Add permission
# verification for account creation", #1401): `is_allowed` splits the
# disjunction so deny-only mode now returns false, and the three call sites
# pass a literal `false` / `deny_only: false` instead of the identity
# comparison. The vulnerable construct is present at 356dc7e^ and the
# remediation at 356dc7e; at a95e549^ both are the patched form, so the row
# as written has no vulnerable revision at all.
#
# ATTENTION, wiring-not-dataflow family (the 1514/1535/1540/1556/1557/1593
# shape, "a flag defaulting open"). The defect is that a permission check is
# skipped, so no tainted value reaches a sink and no taint rule can see it:
# the request body's target_user is labelled
# code.ObjectLookupByUserInput at the get_user lookup, and
# new_service_account/create_user have no sink binding, but the escalation
# happens in the gate's return value, not in any data that moves. The 1380
# counterweight does not reach: the fixed revision does leave the API-documented gate
# call (iam_store.is_allowed / validate_admin_request) inside every guarded
# handler, but it is present verbatim at both revisions, so keying on the
# call discriminates nothing -- the only thing that changed is which value
# one argument carries. The discriminator is the vendor's own vocabulary:
# `deny_only` appears in 10 files of rustfs and nowhere else across the 324
# cloned target repositories (grep over /tmp/cve-work/rank*), so a binding
# keyed on it can never fire on unrelated code and fails the genericity
# test; and the engine-side discriminator would have to be the co-occurrence
# of `deny_only` with `return true` in one statement, which is the fixing
# commit's own layout. The caller-side shape ("an authorization gate
# receives a boolean derived from an identity comparison") is likewise only
# expressible by naming access_key/parent_user/target_user/constant_time_eq,
# and the general form -- a function containing any `==` and any call with
# arguments -- is close to universal in ordinary Rust. Rust emits no `assign:`
# token for `let deny_only = <expr>` at all (probe-measured on the real
# code: node.context.assign contains "deny_only" matches zero functions), so
# the derivation is not linkable to the gate even in principle without
# engine work. Measured on the real code: scanning the tree reports 591
# findings at 356dc7e^ and 591 at 356dc7e, identical sets by fingerprint
# diff (0 added, 0 removed), none on policy.rs, service_account.rs or
# user.rs. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: nothing here is a localized engine
# construct mishandled -- the token routes the caller shape needs do exist
# (`call:`, `binary:`, `selector:`, the body token); what is missing is a
# language-neutral concept for "an authorization gate relaxed by an
# actor/target identity match", which is ontology design, and every spelling
# of it that fits one repository names that repository. No definition change
# shipped. Spec cve_rank1708 records the engine's deny-only grant and the
# self-service caller, both forms, each asserting reject VYQL-AUTH-080: when
# incorrect-authorization vocabulary gains a Rust emitter for a gate whose
# permissive mode is caller-derived, the vulnerable forms must flip to
# expect.

test "rank1708: the policy engine grants access from deny-only mode"
  lang rust
  file crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub enum Effect { Allow, Deny }

  pub struct Statement {
      pub effect: Effect,
      pub action: String,
  }

  pub struct Policy {
      pub statements: Vec<Statement>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  impl Policy {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Deny) && !statement.matches(args).await {
                  return false;
              }
          }

          if args.deny_only || args.is_owner {
              return true;
          }

          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Allow) && statement.matches(args).await {
                  return true;
              }
          }

          false
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: the fixed revision returns denied from deny-only mode"
  lang rust
  file crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub enum Effect { Allow, Deny }

  pub struct Statement {
      pub effect: Effect,
      pub action: String,
  }

  pub struct Policy {
      pub statements: Vec<Statement>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  impl Policy {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          // First, check all Deny statements - if any Deny matches, deny the request
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Deny) && !statement.matches(args).await {
                  return false;
              }
          }

          // Owner has all permissions
          if args.is_owner {
              return true;
          }

          if args.deny_only {
              return false;
          }

          // Check Allow statements
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Allow) && statement.matches(args).await {
                  return true;
              }
          }

          false
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: a service account gate relaxed by an actor/target identity match"
  lang rust
  file rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub struct Credentials {
      pub access_key: String,
      pub parent_user: String,
      pub claims: Option<HashMap<String, String>>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  pub struct IamStore;

  impl IamStore {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          let _ = args;
          true
      }

      pub async fn new_service_account(&self, target_user: &str, opts: &str) -> Credentials {
          Credentials { access_key: target_user.to_string(), parent_user: String::new(), claims: None }
      }
  }

  fn constant_time_eq(a: &str, b: &str) -> bool {
      a.as_bytes() == b.as_bytes()
  }

  pub async fn add_service_account(cred: &Credentials, target_user: &str, owner: bool, iam_store: &IamStore) {
      let deny_only = constant_time_eq(&cred.access_key, target_user) || constant_time_eq(&cred.parent_user, target_user);

      if !iam_store
          .is_allowed(&Args {
              account: &cred.access_key,
              action: "CreateServiceAccountAdminAction",
              is_owner: owner,
              claims: &HashMap::new(),
              deny_only,
          })
          .await
      {
          return;
      }

      let opts = "unrestricted";
      let _ = iam_store.new_service_account(target_user, opts).await;
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: the fixed revision always requires the explicit Allow permission"
  lang rust
  file rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub struct Credentials {
      pub access_key: String,
      pub parent_user: String,
      pub claims: Option<HashMap<String, String>>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  pub struct IamStore;

  impl IamStore {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          let _ = args;
          true
      }

      pub async fn new_service_account(&self, target_user: &str, opts: &str) -> Credentials {
          Credentials { access_key: target_user.to_string(), parent_user: String::new(), claims: None }
      }
  }

  pub async fn add_service_account(cred: &Credentials, target_user: &str, owner: bool, iam_store: &IamStore) {
      // Always require explicit Allow permission
      if !iam_store
          .is_allowed(&Args {
              account: &cred.access_key,
              action: "CreateServiceAccountAdminAction",
              is_owner: owner,
              claims: &HashMap::new(),
              deny_only: false,
          })
          .await
      {
          return;
      }

      let opts = "unrestricted";
      let _ = iam_store.new_service_account(target_user, opts).await;
  }
  ```
